This should give you a working PHP script that interacts with a MySQL database to store data received via an HTTP POST request.

To create a Chrome extension that saves the contents of the current webpage, you'll need to follow these steps:

1. **Set up your project structure**: Create a folder for your extension and inside it, create two files: `manifest.json` and `content.js`.

2. **Create the manifest file (`manifest.json`)**: This file contains metadata about your extension and specifies its permissions and background scripts.

3. **Create the content script (`content.js`)**: This script will be injected into web pages and handle saving their content.

Here's how each part looks like:

### 1. Project Structure
```
my-extension/
├── manifest.json
└── content.js
```

### 2. Manifest File (`manifest.json`)
```json
{
  "manifest_version": 3,
  "name": "Save Page Content",
  "version": "1.0",
  "description": "Saves the contents of the current page.",
  "permissions": [
    "activeTab"
  ],
  "background": {
    "service_worker": "background.js"
  },
  "action": {
    "default_popup": "popup.html",
    "default_icon": {
      "16": "icons/icon16.png",
      "48": "icons/icon48.png",
      "128": "icons/icon128.png"
    }
  },
  "content_scripts": [
    {
      "matches": ["<all_urls>"],
      "js": ["content.js"]
    }
  ]
}
```

### 3. Content Script (`content.js`)
This script captures the HTML content of the current page when triggered by an action (e.g., clicking on the browser action).

```javascript
// Function to save the page content
function savePageContent() {
  const pageTitle = document.title;
  const pageUrl = window.location.href;
  const pageHtml = document.documentElement.outerHTML;

  // Save the content locally or send it via API call
  console.log(`Title: ${pageTitle}`);
  console.log(`URL: ${pageUrl}`);
  console.log(`HTML: ${pageHtml}`);

  // Example: Sending data to a server using fetch
  fetch('https://yourserver.com/save', {
    method: 'POST',
    headers: {
      'Content-Type': 'application/json'
    },
    body: JSON.stringify({
      title: pageTitle,
      url: pageUrl,
      html: pageHtml
    })
  }).then(response => response.json())
    .then(data => {
      console.log('Success:', data);
    })
    .catch((error) => {
      console.error('Error:', error);
    });
}

// Listen for messages from the popup
chrome.runtime.onMessage.addListener((message, sender, sendResponse) => {
  if (message.action === 'save') {
    savePageContent();
  }
});
```

### Additional Files

#### Popup HTML (`popup.html`)
Create this file in the root directory of your extension:
```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
  <meta charset="UTF-8">
  <title>Save Page</title>
  <style>
    body { font-family: Arial, sans-serif; padding: 10px; }
    button { margin-top: 10px; }
  </style>
</head>
<body>
  <h1>Save Current Page</h1>
  <button id="saveButton">Save</button>

  <script src="popup.js"></script>
</body>
</html>
```

#### Popup JavaScript (`popup.js`)
Create this file in the root directory of your extension:
```javascript
document.getElementById('saveButton').addEventListener('click', () => {
  chrome.tabs.query({ active: true, currentWindow: true }, (tabs) => {
    chrome.tabs.sendMessage(tabs[0].id, { action: 'save' });
  });
});
```

### Loading Your Extension
1. Open Chrome and go to `chrome://extensions/`.
2. Enable Developer Mode.
3. Click on "Load unpacked" and select the folder containing your extension files.

Now, whenever you click on the extension icon in the toolbar, it should open a popup with a button labeled "Save". When clicked, it will capture and log the content of the currently active tab. You can modify the `fetch` request to store the data elsewhere as needed.

